| docker stop cont1 | to stop the container |

| docker stop $(docker ps) | to stop all the running containers |

| docker rm $(docker ps -a) | to remove all the stopped containers |

| docker rm cont1 | to remove the container |

# we can also mount the volume with read only permissions

| docker run -itd --name cont1 --mount src=mydata,destination=/pavan,readonly ubuntu |

# named volumes and unnamed volumes. above are the named volumes. unnamed are the ones that we create via the command while creating the container

| docker run -itd --name cont1 -v newvolume:/mypath ubuntu | this will create a new volume called new volume |

| docker run -itd --name cont3 -v /mypath ubuntu | this will create a new unnamed volume but there is no use | we cant rename a volume |

| docker volume | list of volume commands |

# Host to container injecting: Host is where the docker is installed. the data within a path in the host will be replicated in the containers. this can work without creating any volumes. used in dev environments. lets say a git repo is pulled into the host. if we directly mount the path to the container, that code like front end pages is reflected in the containers and the app can be accessed from the browser. in future even if the code is changed, a pull request alone will reflect the changes in the containers too.

